WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.502
-Our first guest tonight is
a talented actress who you know

00:00:03.502 --> 00:00:05.004
from the movies "Lady Bird"
and "Booksmart."

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:07.240
She plays Monica Lewinsky
in the limited series

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.375
"Impeachment:
American Crime Story,"

00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:11.277
which airs Tuesdays, only on FX.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:12.378
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:14.713
-I don't understand why
you have this

00:00:14.713 --> 00:00:16.982
and I don't understand
what is happening.

00:00:16.982 --> 00:00:19.119
Why is this happen...

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:28.328
Linda, what did you do?!

00:00:31.697 --> 00:00:33.566
-Please welcome back to
the show our friend

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:35.335
Beanie Feldstein, everyone.

00:00:35.335 --> 00:00:37.437
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:49.081 --> 00:00:50.616
Welcome back to the show.

00:00:50.616 --> 00:00:52.819
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:54.821
-It's so great to have you here.

00:00:54.821 --> 00:00:56.422
-Oh, my God!
-It's so nice.

00:00:56.422 --> 00:00:58.658
They're so happy to see you.
-Me too.

00:00:58.658 --> 00:01:01.260
-And we're -- I'm not just happy
that you're here at the show.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:02.728
It's also very nice to have you
back in New York City.

00:01:02.728 --> 00:01:04.230
I know this is home.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.398
I know you were away for a long
time working on "Impeachment."

00:01:06.398 --> 00:01:07.466
Are you happy to be back?

00:01:07.466 --> 00:01:09.102
-I'm back in the
New York groove, baby.

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:11.538
I'm here.
I'm so happy to be back.

00:01:11.538 --> 00:01:14.106
I just smile, but no one
can see it under my mask.

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:15.607
When I walk around, I'm just
like, "I'm so happy to be here."

00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:17.109
-Your eyes. There's a light in
your eyes when you smile.

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:20.913
-There's, like, a real smizing
happening in my eyes, for sure.

00:01:20.913 --> 00:01:22.414
-And we've talked in the past.

00:01:22.414 --> 00:01:24.650
Obviously, this is not a secret

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:26.752
that you are a great fan of
musical theater.

00:01:26.752 --> 00:01:28.688
Have you been able go back
and see shows yet?

00:01:28.688 --> 00:01:30.089
Have done it?
-Yes.

00:01:30.089 --> 00:01:33.225
My partner, Bon,
had never seen "Hamilton."

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:35.628
So, I was like, "I have to take
you to see 'Hamilton.'"

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:37.296
And she's British,
so she loved the king.

00:01:37.296 --> 00:01:40.666
I was like, "That's not
the takeaway of 'Hamilton.'"

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:44.337
She was like, "Oh, yay!"

00:01:44.337 --> 00:01:45.471
But we had the greatest time.

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:46.772
We saw Freestyle Love Supreme.

00:01:46.772 --> 00:01:48.274
We saw "Nollywood Dreams"
at MCC.

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:50.309
It's been so great to be back
at the theater.

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:51.610
-That's really exciting.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:54.847
And we were talking backstage
about "Funny Girl."

00:01:54.847 --> 00:01:58.551
I mean, this is a show
that has never been revived.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:02.221
An iconic part played by
Barbra Streisand.

00:02:02.221 --> 00:02:03.256
-It's Barbra Streisand.
-It's Barbra Streisand, but --

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:05.057
-It's just Barbra Streisand.

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:07.560
-You know, so you must have --
I mean, I don't know if

00:02:07.560 --> 00:02:09.461
hesitation's the right word,
but, like, what's it like

00:02:09.461 --> 00:02:11.464
to take on something like that?

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:12.965
-It's insane.

00:02:12.965 --> 00:02:17.670
I mean, it's truly
the first dream I ever had.

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:21.741
"Funny Girl" was my
obsession as a 2-year-old.

00:02:21.741 --> 00:02:23.209
Normal.

00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:27.313
Like, I begged my mom to make my
third birthday party

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:29.915
"Funny Girl"-themed.

00:02:29.915 --> 00:02:31.917
So, you know, like,
at Party City, you have, like,

00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:34.620
Elmo plates and
"Beauty and the Beast."

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:38.991
And I was like, "Where is the
Fanny Brice balloons?"

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:41.027
And she was like,
"They don't exist."

00:02:41.027 --> 00:02:42.195
It didn't click for me.

00:02:42.195 --> 00:02:43.996
But I literally had
a "Funny Girl"-themed

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:44.997
birthday party.

00:02:44.997 --> 00:02:47.567
My mom made me a
Fanny Brice costume.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:50.169
-So, you're 3 years old.
That's so shocking to me.

00:02:50.169 --> 00:02:51.403
Like, how did you know it?

00:02:51.403 --> 00:02:52.538
Were your parents
just playing, like,

00:02:52.538 --> 00:02:54.073
the Broadway recording, like --

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:55.541
-My mom showed me the movie.

00:02:55.541 --> 00:02:56.642
She was like, "Look at this

00:02:56.642 --> 00:02:58.511
iconic Jewish woman
on the screen.

00:02:58.511 --> 00:03:00.079
You have to see this at
2 years old."

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.915
And I became obsessed with it
and I would ask her to play it.

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:04.917
Like, other kids would be like,
"Play 'The Little Mermaid.'"

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:07.654
I'd be like, "Can we do
'Don't Rain On My Parade'

00:03:07.654 --> 00:03:10.756
one more time, please, Mom,
from the top?"

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:12.191
And I just became
obsessed with it.

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:14.093
So, yeah, I'd walk around
my third birthday party, like,

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:16.428
eating all my snacks in my,
you know, Fanny Brice costume.

00:03:16.428 --> 00:03:17.797
And all the parents
are laughing,

00:03:17.797 --> 00:03:19.064
and all the kids are like,
"What the heck is going on?"

00:03:19.064 --> 00:03:20.366
-"What's going on?"

00:03:20.366 --> 00:03:22.201
What about --
So, then, it must have been

00:03:22.201 --> 00:03:23.903
a pretty special moment
when you told your mom

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:25.037
you were going to do
this on Broadway.

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:26.372
What was that like?

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.440
-So, I didn't tell my mom
that I was auditioning,

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:30.809
'cause I was like,
"If this doesn't go my way,

00:03:30.809 --> 00:03:32.645
it's going to hurt her
so much more

00:03:32.645 --> 00:03:33.946
than it's going to hurt me."

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:36.515
So she had no idea
any of this was happening.

00:03:36.515 --> 00:03:38.985
So, when I got the call
that I was going to be

00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:42.321
Fanny Brice on Broadway, which
is insane, I called my mom.

00:03:42.321 --> 00:03:43.555
And it was during the pandemic.

00:03:43.555 --> 00:03:45.124
It was -- Obviously,
we're still in a pandemic,

00:03:45.124 --> 00:03:46.525
but it was pre-vaccine.

00:03:46.525 --> 00:03:48.594
And I called her and I was like,
"I'm going to come by the house.

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:51.664
Please put your mask on.
And bring dad, come outside.

00:03:51.664 --> 00:03:52.698
I'm going to be masked.

00:03:52.698 --> 00:03:53.766
But I have something
to tell you."

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:54.867
My mom was like,
"What's going on?

00:03:54.867 --> 00:03:56.502
What? What is going on?"

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:58.805
Like, screaming.
Like, literally shaking.

00:03:58.805 --> 00:03:59.905
And she's like,
"You just have to tell me,

00:03:59.905 --> 00:04:00.973
because I can't sit here."

00:04:00.973 --> 00:04:01.974
I'm like,
"It's been four seconds.

00:04:01.974 --> 00:04:03.175
You need to calm down."

00:04:03.175 --> 00:04:04.510
-Now, do you think -- I don't
know your mom.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.378
Do you think she thought she was
about to hear good news

00:04:06.378 --> 00:04:07.446
or do you think she was worried

00:04:07.446 --> 00:04:08.747
that it was going to be
bad news?

00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:10.683
-I mean, it's Jewish mom.
It's, like, gonna go to the bad.

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:11.951
You know, whatever.

00:04:11.951 --> 00:04:13.385
She was like,
"Something is happening

00:04:13.385 --> 00:04:15.120
that I don't know about."

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.255
-That's really what it is.

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:17.857
Good or bad, it's like,
"I don't know!"

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:19.625
-"I'm not in the know."

00:04:19.625 --> 00:04:24.864
And I was like, "I'm going to be
Fanny Brice on Broadway."

00:04:24.864 --> 00:04:27.300
And I've never seen her
that happy.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:29.568
It was honestly, like --
And even my dad --

00:04:29.568 --> 00:04:32.138
[ Cheers and applause ]
Oh, thank you.

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:34.173
Even my dad, who's, like --

00:04:34.173 --> 00:04:36.442
I think my dad says like
10 words a year.

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:38.544
Like, he literally
doesn't speak.

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:42.815
Was like,
"Bean, that is incredible."

00:04:42.815 --> 00:04:46.352
And I was like -- So even he was
excited in his own way, yeah.

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:49.455
-I say -- This is a comment
not on your mother,

00:04:49.455 --> 00:04:51.623
but on my mother and things that
she has done in the past.

00:04:51.623 --> 00:04:53.992
How quickly after
you gave her the good news

00:04:53.992 --> 00:04:56.361
did she start asking for
tickets for her friends?

00:04:56.361 --> 00:04:58.063
-It was,
"Can I tell my friends?"

00:04:58.063 --> 00:04:59.132
And I was like, "No.

00:04:59.132 --> 00:05:00.532
And you can't tell them
for months."

00:05:00.532 --> 00:05:03.402
And she was like, "That's going
to be hard for me."

00:05:03.402 --> 00:05:05.971
That's going to be tough for me,
because I really wanted to

00:05:05.971 --> 00:05:08.941
call -- and if I can't call --"
I'm like, "Okay, relax."

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:10.943
But, yes, the tickets -- she
calls me once a day about them.

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.946
I'm like, "I promise
you're going to get to go.

00:05:13.946 --> 00:05:15.814
Like, it's not a problem,
I swear."

00:05:15.814 --> 00:05:19.151
-"I'm sorry, Mom. It's sold out.
I'm sorry."

00:05:19.151 --> 00:05:21.019
We talked about this,
when you were in "Hello, Dolly!"

00:05:21.019 --> 00:05:24.089
Your brother, Jonah Hill,
got a "Hello, Beanie" tattoo,

00:05:24.089 --> 00:05:28.127
which is just such an incredibly
loving and supporting thing.

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:30.396
-Insane. When he told me he was
going to do it, I was like,

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:33.399
"Get it on your arm," and I
expected it to be like that big.

00:05:33.399 --> 00:05:34.967
And it's, like,
truly most of his --

00:05:34.967 --> 00:05:37.770
-It's a real big one, yeah.
-Yeah, it's too big, honestly.

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:38.771
-And do we think there's
a "Funny Girl" tattoo

00:05:38.771 --> 00:05:39.772
in his future?

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:41.106
I feel like --

00:05:41.106 --> 00:05:42.241
-You have to keep up --
If you start this rolling --

00:05:42.241 --> 00:05:43.876
-You got to keep up
with the revivals, yeah.

00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:45.210
-Yeah, exactly.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.146
If you're not going to put
the revivals on your skin,

00:05:47.146 --> 00:05:48.447
where are you --
What are you gonna --

00:05:48.447 --> 00:05:50.850
So, yeah, I guess there has to
be some sort of, like --

00:05:50.850 --> 00:05:52.785
-I mean, it does seem, like,
very complimentary.

00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:53.786
-It's very sweet.

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:55.087
-I mean, he said the
"Hello, Beanie,"

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:56.389
so does he do like,
"Funny Beanie"

00:05:56.389 --> 00:05:57.389
or does he do "Beanie Girl?"

00:05:57.389 --> 00:05:58.390
-"Beanie Girl"?
Like, what is it?

00:05:58.390 --> 00:05:59.625
I don't know.

00:05:59.625 --> 00:06:02.194
I'm not that funny, so,
you know, it has to be --

00:06:02.194 --> 00:06:03.662
-This must also
be nice to, like,

00:06:03.662 --> 00:06:05.965
go into something as joyous
as this after --

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:08.901
While it's an
incredible performance,

00:06:08.901 --> 00:06:11.437
"Impeachment"
must have been exhausting,

00:06:11.437 --> 00:06:13.773
both the schedule
and just the emotional weight

00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:14.773
of what you're playing.

00:06:14.773 --> 00:06:15.842
-Yeah, I mean, I was standing

00:06:15.842 --> 00:06:17.309
backstage when you were
playing the clip

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:19.345
and I'm like --
You know, it's just --

00:06:19.345 --> 00:06:21.880
It's so --
It was really something like

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:23.182
I had never done before.

00:06:23.182 --> 00:06:25.217
Most of what I've been
lucky enough to be a part of

00:06:25.217 --> 00:06:27.586
in the past
has been leaning comedic,

00:06:27.586 --> 00:06:32.191
you know, like a dramedy or like
a true comedy or a musical.

00:06:32.191 --> 00:06:34.693
And "Impeachment"
is not only a drama,

00:06:34.693 --> 00:06:36.161
but it's someone's real life.

00:06:36.161 --> 00:06:38.397
And Monica was a producer
on the show.

00:06:38.397 --> 00:06:42.701
So it was a tremendous amount of
responsibility for me.

00:06:42.701 --> 00:06:47.106
I really felt like
I wanted to do right by her

00:06:47.106 --> 00:06:48.507
in every way
that I possibly could.

00:06:48.507 --> 00:06:51.244
And so to do that,
I had to really send myself

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:53.045
into some really intense places.

00:06:53.045 --> 00:06:54.380
And Ryan Murphy directed
the episode

00:06:54.380 --> 00:06:55.514
from the clip you just saw.

00:06:55.514 --> 00:06:58.350
And he'd come up to me and
he would be like,

00:06:58.350 --> 00:06:59.418
"How you doing?

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:00.486
Can we go again?"

00:07:00.486 --> 00:07:01.487
And I just would be like,
"It's for Monica.

00:07:01.487 --> 00:07:02.755
We have to go again.

00:07:02.755 --> 00:07:05.624
If you need to go again,
I have to do right by her."

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:06.958
That's, like, all I --

00:07:06.958 --> 00:07:08.461
I brush my teeth
thinking about it.

00:07:08.461 --> 00:07:09.529
I go to sleep thinking about it.

00:07:09.529 --> 00:07:11.197
Like, I do --
Everything in my life

00:07:11.197 --> 00:07:12.932
revolves around
doing right by her.

00:07:12.932 --> 00:07:14.667
So I -- You know, it's --
What she had to

00:07:14.667 --> 00:07:17.203
go through was
really disturbing and intense.

00:07:17.203 --> 00:07:19.471
So, yeah, it was very different.

00:07:19.471 --> 00:07:21.573
-It's been very nice to sort of
correct the record,

00:07:21.573 --> 00:07:23.676
because I think a lot of people,
especially, you know,

00:07:23.676 --> 00:07:25.277
younger people,
not that, you know,

00:07:25.277 --> 00:07:27.212
older people shouldn't be
excused either,

00:07:27.212 --> 00:07:28.747
but I think younger people might
not know the full story

00:07:28.747 --> 00:07:31.117
and might know it from,
like, small pieces of, like,

00:07:31.117 --> 00:07:33.185
you know, jokes or this or that.

00:07:33.185 --> 00:07:34.887
And to actually see
what happened --

00:07:34.887 --> 00:07:36.421
I would imagine,
when you would talk to Monica,

00:07:36.421 --> 00:07:38.291
who, as you mentioned,
was a producer on it --

00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:40.258
Like, what sort of questions
would you ask her

00:07:40.258 --> 00:07:44.029
without having to make her
sort of relive it herself?

00:07:44.029 --> 00:07:45.664
-Totally. So, she's the producer
and the subject,

00:07:45.664 --> 00:07:49.335
so that's, like, a really
tricky thing to navigate.

00:07:49.335 --> 00:07:50.869
And, as you said,
I didn't want to make her

00:07:50.869 --> 00:07:53.438
sort of rehash the most
traumatic days of her life.

00:07:53.438 --> 00:07:55.541
So, by the time I met her,
I'd been researching her

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:57.843
for 10 months, which is, like,
a really weird interaction

00:07:57.843 --> 00:08:01.180
of like, "Hello, I know almost
everything about you."

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.983
And, so, I really wanted know
things that I just

00:08:03.983 --> 00:08:05.784
couldn't find in any
book or any transcript,

00:08:05.784 --> 00:08:08.720
because Monica was
interviewed 22 times

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:11.156
by the Office of Independent
Counsel about what happened.

00:08:11.156 --> 00:08:12.691
So, unfortunately, there's just

00:08:12.691 --> 00:08:15.861
so much material
of her being questioned.

00:08:15.861 --> 00:08:17.129
So I wanted things that, like,
you'll just never

00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:18.297
be able to find.

00:08:18.297 --> 00:08:19.865
Like, her best friend's name
is Catherine.

00:08:19.865 --> 00:08:21.600
I was like, "Is she Cat?
Is she Cathy?

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:22.734
Like, when the two of you
are just, like,

00:08:22.734 --> 00:08:25.204
alone and hanging,
like, who is she to you?"

00:08:25.204 --> 00:08:28.006
You know, these things that I
just couldn't get from any book.

00:08:28.006 --> 00:08:30.943
Like, is that Grandma Bubby or
is that Grandma Bubby?

00:08:30.943 --> 00:08:32.944
You know, just, like,
these little specificities

00:08:32.944 --> 00:08:34.380
that I couldn't find.

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:37.516
And she was so gracious and she
was so giving to all of us

00:08:37.516 --> 00:08:40.419
from every stage of the project,
and I just --

00:08:40.419 --> 00:08:41.920
Yeah, I hope we
did right by her.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:43.956
-I think you did.
Congratulations on that.

00:08:43.956 --> 00:08:45.324
I'm so excited for "Funny Girl."

00:08:45.324 --> 00:08:46.392
And thanks so much
for being here.

00:08:46.392 --> 00:08:47.726
-Thank you for having me.
I'm so happy.

00:08:47.726 --> 00:08:49.462
-It's always a delight.
Beanie Feldstein, everybody.

00:08:49.462 --> 00:08:50.729
"Impeachment:
American Crime Story,"

00:08:50.729 --> 00:08:51.731
airs Tuesdays on FX.

